@startuml

title Reusable Bilateral FX Atomic Swap via a Shared ERC-20 Settlement Router\nEach example trade: Party A receives BASE_QTY base tokens and pays QUOTE_QTY quote tokens

autonumber
hide footbox
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam responseMessageBelowArrow true

actor "Deployer" as Deployer
actor "Registry owner\n(proposed route)" as RegistryOwner #FFF2CC
actor "Party A\n(inceptor)" as PartyA
actor "Party B\n(confirmer)" as PartyB
participant "ProductDefinitionRegistry\n(proposed route)" as Registry #FFF2CC
participant "Factory\nstandalone: FxAtomicSwapFactory\nproposed: FxAtomicSwapProductFactory" as Factory
participant "ERC20AtomicSwapSettlementRouter\n(common spender)" as Router
participant "FxAtomicSwap\n(reusable bilateral instance)" as Swap
participant "Base ERC-20" as BaseToken
participant "Quote ERC-20" as QuoteToken

== Factory and settlement router deployment ==

note over RegistryOwner, Factory #FFF2CC
The framework route is a proposal, not implemented Solidity.
Select one factory deployment: standalone OR framework-aware.
The proposed factory extends AbstractProductFactory and directly deploys FX swaps.
It owns its router; it does not forward through the standalone factory.
end note

alt Standalone deployment (implemented)
Deployer -> Factory : new FxAtomicSwapFactory()
else Framework deployment (proposed)
Deployer -> Factory : new FxAtomicSwapProductFactory(Registry)
end
activate Factory
create Router
Factory -> Router ++ : new ERC20AtomicSwapSettlementRouter(address(Factory))
Router -> Router : factory = Factory\n(immutable)
Router --> Factory -- : router deployed
Factory -> Factory : settlementRouter = Router\n(immutable)
Factory --> Deployer -- : factory deployed

opt Proposed framework route: publish an FX product definition once
RegistryOwner -> Registry ++ : publishProductDefinition({name,\n  productDefinition: {Factory, termsTemplate, documentationTemplate}})
Registry -> Factory ++ : check IProductFactory via ERC-165, configured registry,\n  payload schema and required resource types
Factory --> Registry -- : factory configuration
Registry -> Registry : validate finalized terms/documentation resources\nstore definition; emit ProductDefinitionPublished
Registry --> RegistryOwner -- : productDefinitionId
note over RegistryOwner, Factory #FFF2CC
Registry and finalized text resources are already deployed.
No indication assignment or trade-request workflow is required for this route.
Resource validation calls are summarized above.
end note
end

== ERC-20 allowances ==

note over PartyA, QuoteToken
Allowances are given early to the deployed settlementRouter.
Swaps created through the selected factory settle through its settlementRouter.
Tokens must transfer the exact requested amount synchronously.
Fee-on-transfer and rebasing tokens are outside this example's supported scope.
end note

PartyA -> QuoteToken ++ : approve(Router, quoteAllowance)
QuoteToken -> QuoteToken : allowance[PartyA][Router] = quoteAllowance
QuoteToken --> PartyA -- : true

PartyB -> BaseToken ++ : approve(Router, baseAllowance)
BaseToken -> BaseToken : allowance[PartyB][Router] = baseAllowance
BaseToken --> PartyB -- : true

note over PartyA, QuoteToken
For this sign configuration, Party A authorizes quote-token spending
and Party B authorizes base-token spending. Any remaining allowance can be used
by later trades on this instance or other swaps registered with the same router.
Replenish allowances before later settlements when necessary.
end note

== Create and register one reusable instance ==

alt Standalone creation (implemented)
PartyA -> Factory : createSwap(BaseToken, QuoteToken, PartyA, PartyB)
Factory -> Factory : require caller is PartyA or PartyB
else Framework issuance (proposed alternative)
RegistryOwner -> Registry : issueProductWithoutRequests({productDefinitionId,\n  productPayload: abi.encode(BaseToken, QuoteToken, PartyA, PartyB)})
Registry -> Registry : require registry owner and fulfillable definition\nassemble immutable definition context
Registry -> Factory : deployProduct({common, productPayload})
Factory -> Factory : require caller and common.productRegistry match Registry\ndecode BaseToken, QuoteToken, PartyA, PartyB
end

create Swap
Factory -> Swap ++ : new FxAtomicSwap(\n  BaseToken, QuoteToken, PartyA, PartyB, Router\n)
Swap -> Swap : store immutable tokens, parties, and Router\nphase = Created
Swap --> Factory -- : swap address
Factory -> Router ++ : registerSwap(Swap)
Router -> Router : require msg.sender == Factory\nrequire Swap has code and is not registered\nregisteredSwap[Swap] = true
Router --> Factory -- : registered

alt Standalone return (implemented)
Factory -> Factory : emit SwapCreated(Swap, PartyA, BaseToken, QuoteToken, PartyA, PartyB)
Factory --> PartyA : Swap address
else Framework registration (proposed alternative)
Factory -> Factory : record factory provenance\nemit ProductDeployed(common.productProvider, productDefinitionId, Swap)
Factory --> Registry : Swap address
Registry -> Factory ++ : isFactoryDeployedProduct(Swap)
Factory --> Registry -- : true
Registry -> Registry : verify deployed code and provenance\nrecord registered product; emit ProductIssued
Registry --> RegistryOwner : Swap address
note over RegistryOwner, Swap #FFF2CC
Issuance creates and registers the reusable instance once.
Later trades use that same instance directly, without registry issuance.
Issuance does not authorize a trade or move tokens.
The no-request route does not automatically check client eligibility.
The existing party/operator agreement below remains required.
The current registry does not require IProduct metadata getters on the swap.
end note
end

note over Swap, Router
The router permanently registers the instance and consumes trade IDs individually.
The swap fixes tokens, parties and router; amounts and terms belong to each trade.
Either fixed party may incept. The example below consistently uses Party A.
end note

note over PartyA, Swap
The arrows below show direct participant calls.
A participant may call setTradeOperator(operator, true) on this swap.
A's approved operator may submit inception or cancellation; B's may confirm.
Caller identity does not change either participant or the amount perspective.
Approval is checked at execution and may be revoked with false.
Operator approval also applies to future trades on this instance until revoked.
ERC-20 allowances alone do not authorize trade actions.
end note

loop Successive trades on this instance: one pending quote at a time

== ERC-6123 inception generates a fresh trade ID ==

PartyA -> Swap ++ : inceptTrade(\n  PartyA, PartyB, tradeData,\n  +BASE_QTY, -QUOTE_QTY, settlementData\n)
Swap -> Swap : require no pending quote\nvalidate explicit fixed parties\nauthorize caller for PartyA\nvalidate nonzero, opposite-sign legs; reject int256 minimum
Swap -> Swap : generate next monotonic decimal tradeId\n("1", "2", ...; never reused)\nstore record under tradeId:\n  inceptor = PartyA, position = +BASE_QTY\n  paymentAmount = -QUOTE_QTY, textual terms\n  phase = Incepted\nset latest tradeId
Swap -> Swap : emit TradeIncepted(PartyA, PartyB, tradeId, tradeData,\n  +BASE_QTY, -QUOTE_QTY, settlementData)
Swap --> PartyA -- : generated tradeId (return value)

note over PartyA, Swap
On-chain clients use the returned ID. Off-chain clients read TradeIncepted
from their transaction receipt; do not predict an ID before submission.
The local demo reads tradeId() after successful inception with no intervening trade.
Confirmation and cancellation include the actual generated ID.
end note

note right of Swap
Sign convention from the inceptor's perspective:
  position > 0       => receive base
  paymentAmount < 0  => deliver quote
The rate follows from the two exact amounts and token decimals.
Each trade can use different amounts and textual terms.
end note

== Resolve the quote through ERC-6123 confirmation or cancellation ==

alt Party B confirms successfully before cancellation

PartyB -> Swap ++ : confirmTrade(\n  same tradeId, PartyA, PartyB, same tradeData,\n  +BASE_QTY, -QUOTE_QTY, same settlementData\n)
Swap -> Swap : match pending tradeId; require phase == Incepted\nmatch both stored parties\nauthorize caller for PartyB\nmatch original signed amounts and terms
Swap -> Swap : trade phase = Settled\n(checks-effects-interactions)
Swap -> Router ++ : settleAtomicSwap(\n  tradeId, BaseToken, QuoteToken, PartyA, PartyB,\n  +BASE_QTY, -QUOTE_QTY\n)

note over Swap, Router
Inside the router, msg.sender = Swap.
Permanent registration authenticates the factory-created caller.
end note

Router -> Router : require registeredSwap[Swap]\nrequire nonempty tradeId\nidHash = keccak256(bytes(tradeId))\nrequire !consumedTrade[Swap][idHash]\nconsumedTrade[Swap][idHash] = true

note over Router, Swap
Consume this ID before external token calls to prevent duplicate settlement.
The swap guards inception, confirmation and cancellation against reentrancy,
including attempts to start another trade during the current settlement.
end note

note over Router, QuoteToken
The included tokens return true, as shown below.
The router also accepts empty return data. A revert, false return,
or invalid ABI-encoded boolean causes settlement to revert.
end note

group Base leg: Party B delivers base to Party A
    Router -> BaseToken ++ : transferFrom(PartyB, PartyA, BASE_QTY)
    note over Router, BaseToken
    Inside BaseToken, msg.sender = Router.
    Therefore allowance[PartyB][Router] is used.
    end note
    BaseToken -> BaseToken : check allowance and balance\nupdate balances and decrease finite allowance
    BaseToken --> Router -- : true
end

group Quote leg: Party A delivers quote to Party B
    Router -> QuoteToken ++ : transferFrom(PartyA, PartyB, QUOTE_QTY)
    note over Router, QuoteToken
    Inside QuoteToken, msg.sender = Router.
    Therefore allowance[PartyA][Router] is used.
    end note
    QuoteToken -> QuoteToken : check allowance and balance\nupdate balances and decrease finite allowance
    QuoteToken --> Router -- : true
end

Router --> Swap -- : settlement completed
Swap -> Swap : emit TradeConfirmed(PartyB, tradeId)\nemit TradeTerminated(tradeId, "atomic settlement completed")
Swap --> PartyB -- : confirmation transaction succeeds

note over PartyA, QuoteToken
Settlement occurs in one transaction submitted by Party B or its approved operator.
If validation or either token call fails, all effects of that transaction are undone.
For the open quote shown here, phase remains Incepted and consumedTrade = false:
Party B may retry, or Party A may cancel, directly or through their approved operators.
A rejected call for a closed ID preserves its terminal state and any newer trade.
end note

else Party A cancels before successful confirmation

PartyA -> Swap ++ : cancelTrade(\n  same tradeId, PartyA, PartyB, same tradeData,\n  +BASE_QTY, -QUOTE_QTY, same settlementData\n)
Swap -> Swap : match pending tradeId; require phase == Incepted\nmatch both stored parties\nauthorize caller for PartyA\nmatch all original terms
Swap -> Swap : trade phase = Canceled\n(checks-effects-interactions)
Swap -> Router ++ : cancelAtomicSwap(tradeId)
Router -> Router : require registeredSwap[Swap]\nrequire nonempty tradeId\nidHash = keccak256(bytes(tradeId))\nrequire !consumedTrade[Swap][idHash]\nconsumedTrade[Swap][idHash] = true
Router --> Swap -- : this trade ID consumed
Swap -> Swap : emit TradeCanceled(PartyA, tradeId)
Swap --> PartyA -- : cancellation transaction succeeds

note over PartyA, QuoteToken
Cancellation transfers no tokens and preserves balances and ERC-20 allowances.
If the router call fails, cancellation reverts completely.
end note

end

note over PartyA, Swap
This trade ID is permanently closed; another inception generates a fresh ID.
No factory call, router re-registration or registry issuance is needed.
Latest getters and the ERC-8100 XML view follow the latest trade.
getTrade(id) retains each trade's terms and phase; closed records remain unchanged.
end note

end

note over PartyA, QuoteToken
An open quote is withdrawn through cancelTrade by the inceptor or its approved operator.
There is no clock-based expiry; transaction order determines which terminal action succeeds.
Settled and Canceled are final for one ID, while the instance remains reusable.
Router registration persists; per-ID consumption flags and stored records are never reset.
The request/confirm/cancelTradeTermination methods are unsupported and revert.
end note

@enduml
